# Kubernetes Dashboard

## What changed

As of Dashboard **v7.0.0** the manifest install is gone. This no longer works:

```
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/dashboard/v2.7.0/aio/deploy/recommended.yaml
```

Dashboard is now a multi-container application with a Kong gateway in front
of it, and the project supports Helm only. The old `recommended.yaml` that
used to sit in this repo has been removed for the same reason — it installed
v2.7.0, which is years out of support.

## Install

```
helm repo add kubernetes-dashboard https://kubernetes.github.io/dashboard/

helm upgrade --install kubernetes-dashboard \
  kubernetes-dashboard/kubernetes-dashboard \
  --create-namespace --namespace kubernetes-dashboard
```

## Create a user to log in with

Dashboard has no users of its own. You authenticate with a ServiceAccount
token.

```
kubectl apply -f service_account.yaml
kubectl apply -f ClusterRole_Bind_to_Service_Account.yaml

kubectl -n kubernetes-dashboard create token admin-user
```

Copy the token. It is valid for one hour by default; add `--duration=8h` if
you are running a full-day class.

> The binding in `ClusterRole_Bind_to_Service_Account.yaml` grants
> **cluster-admin**. That is fine for a lab cluster and wrong everywhere
> else — it is full control of the cluster, handed to anyone who gets the
> token. For a real cluster, bind the `view` ClusterRole instead and create
> separate, narrower roles for the people who need to change things.

## Access it

```
kubectl -n kubernetes-dashboard port-forward svc/kubernetes-dashboard-kong-proxy 8443:443
```

Then open <https://localhost:8443> and paste the token.

Use `port-forward` rather than patching the Service to NodePort. A NodePort
exposes the dashboard on every node in the cluster, to anyone who can reach
them, and it tends to outlive the class it was created for.
